      "side": 1,
      "message": "I haven\u0027t looked at the whole file so let me know if this doesn\u0027t work for everything but I notice that most functions take a string literal so what about having one or two type for any generic libfdt error e.g.\n\n- `FdtValidationError::FdtNodeError(\u0026\u0027static CStr, FdtError)`\n- `FdtValidationError::FdtPropError(\u0026\u0027static CStr, \u0026\u0027static CStr, FdtError)` \n\nthat could be used as\n\n```\nfdt.compatible_nodes(compatible)\n   .map_err(|e| FdtValidationError::FdtNodeError(compatible, e))?\n```\n```\nnode.getprop_cells(prop)\n    .map_err(|e| FdtValidationError::FdtPropError(compatible, prop, e))?\n```\n\nwhich could perhaps be wrapped in functions?\n\nI\u0027m not convinced this will result in less verbose code but the error type should be pretty versatile, WDYT?\n\nAlternatively, we could maybe have one `FdtError`-wrapping `FdtValidationError` variant per node type e.g. `FdtValidationError::CpuError(e)`, `FdtValidationError::KernelError(e)`, `FdtValidationError::RamdiskError(e)`, ... to get an error message referring to the cause node (but not property, which is probably overkill anyway) without the verbosity at the call-site?",
